# Forest Cover Type Classification

## Project Overview

This project focuses on developing and evaluating machine learning classification models to predict forest cover types using cartographic and environmental features.

The project uses the UCI Covertype dataset, which contains geographical and environmental measurements collected from forest areas. Seven forest cover types are represented in the target variable.

The primary objective is to compare different classification algorithms and identify models that provide reliable predictions across all seven classes.

## Objectives

- Explore and understand the Forest Cover Type dataset.
- Prepare the dataset for multiclass classification.
- Develop Decision Tree, Random Forest and XGBoost models.
- Address class imbalance and investigate its effect on classification performance.
- Evaluate models using Accuracy, Balanced Accuracy, Precision, Recall and Macro F1-score.
- Apply hyperparameter tuning to improve model performance.
- Compare the models and identify the strongest-performing configurations.

## Dataset Description

**Dataset:** UCI Covertype Dataset

**Source:** [UCI Machine Learning Repository – Covertype](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/31/covertype)

The dataset contains:

- 581,012 observations.
- 54 input features.
- 1 target variable (`Cover_Type`).
- 7 forest cover classes.

The input features include elevation, aspect, slope, distances to hydrology, roadways and fire points, hillshade measurements, wilderness area indicators and soil type indicators.

The wilderness area and soil type features were already one-hot encoded in the dataset.

## Technologies and Libraries

- Python
- Google Colab
- Pandas
- NumPy
- Matplotlib
- Seaborn
- Scikit-learn
- XGBoost

## Methodology

### 1. Data Exploration and Preparation

The dataset was examined to understand its structure, feature types, class distribution and data quality.

The investigation identified no missing values or duplicate records. The target variable showed class imbalance, with some forest cover types having substantially fewer observations than others.

### 2. Data Splitting

The dataset was divided into:

- Training set: 80%
- Testing set: 20%

Stratified sampling was applied to preserve the class distribution across the training and testing sets.

### 3. Model Development

Six model configurations were evaluated:

1. Decision Tree
2. Random Forest
3. Original XGBoost
4. Class-balanced XGBoost
5. Tuned Random Forest
6. Tuned XGBoost

Class weighting and sample weighting were explored to address class imbalance.

### 4. Hyperparameter Tuning

RandomizedSearchCV was used to investigate different hyperparameter combinations for Random Forest and XGBoost.

The search used:
- 20% of the training data as the tuning sample.
- 8 randomly selected parameter combinations per algorithm.
- 3-fold cross-validation.
- Macro F1-score as the tuning objective.

The selected configurations were subsequently retrained on the full training dataset and evaluated using the independent test dataset.

## Experimental Results

The following results were obtained on the test dataset.

| Model | Accuracy | Balanced Accuracy | Macro F1-score |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Decision Tree | 93.62% | 88.52% | 89.17% |
| Random Forest | 95.45% | 90.47% | 92.29% |
| Original XGBoost | 85.53% | 79.44% | 82.34% |
| Balanced XGBoost | 85.95% | 92.16% | 82.73% |
| Tuned Random Forest | 95.08% | 92.86% | 92.27% |
| Tuned XGBoost | 94.73% | 92.17% | 93.17% |

### Key Findings

- The original Random Forest achieved the highest overall accuracy of 95.45%.
- Tuned Random Forest achieved the highest Balanced Accuracy of 92.86%.
- Tuned XGBoost achieved the highest Macro F1-score of 93.17%.
- Class-balanced XGBoost improved minority-class recognition, although its overall accuracy remained lower.
- Hyperparameter tuning improved the Macro F1-score of XGBoost from 82.34% to 93.17%.

These findings demonstrate the importance of evaluating multiclass models using multiple metrics, particularly when the dataset is imbalanced.

## Feature Importance

Feature importance analysis was performed using the Random Forest model.

Elevation was identified as the most influential feature, followed by horizontal distance to roadways, horizontal distance to fire points and horizontal distance to hydrology.

These results describe the model's reliance on the input features and should not be interpreted as proof of causal relationships.

## Conclusion

The experiments demonstrate that tree-based ensemble methods can achieve strong performance in forest cover type classification.

Although the original Random Forest achieved the highest accuracy, tuned Random Forest provided the highest balanced accuracy, while tuned XGBoost achieved the highest Macro F1-score.

Tuned XGBoost is therefore a strong candidate when balanced precision and recall across all seven classes are important. Tuned Random Forest is also a strong candidate when overall class recall is the priority.

The project highlights the value of class imbalance handling, hyperparameter tuning and comprehensive model evaluation in multiclass classification problems.

## Limitations

- The experiment used one train/test split, so performance may vary with different random splits.
- Hyperparameter tuning explored a limited number of randomly selected configurations.
- The dataset represents specific forest conditions and may not generalise perfectly to other geographical regions.
- Feature importance is model-dependent and does not establish causation.
- The study evaluated predictive performance rather than deployment in a live forest monitoring system.

## Future Improvements

- Perform repeated stratified cross-validation.
- Explore additional ensemble and boosting algorithms.
- Investigate more extensive hyperparameter optimisation.
- Evaluate model performance on data from different geographical regions.
- Explore deployment through a simple web-based prediction application.

